judge_raw_output:
The analyzer shows the highest-priority problem is the quality_score being massively out of range (mean 4.7902e8 ± 3.2603e7, best 4.8517e8) which will dominate selection despite being invalid. To avoid wasting iterations while the scorer is fixed, reduce its influence and steer optimization toward improving parameter_mean (current transformed mean 0.6782 ± 0.0638), which the analyzer flagged as needing more emphasis.

<decision>{
  "failure_location": "implementation",
  "root_cause_hypothesis": "The quality_score scorer is producing out-of-range (very large) values due to a scorer implementation or reporting/scaling bug, causing it to dominate selection.",
  "intervention_target": "objective",
  "rationale": "quality_score mean reported 4.7902e8 ± 3.2603e7 (best 4.8517e8) is orders of magnitude outside expected [0,1], so it likely distorts selection. parameter_mean is at 0.6782 ± 0.0638 and needs stronger emphasis to drive candidates toward the 0.5 mean target. Temporarily down-weighting the mis-scaled quality_score will let the valid parameter objectives guide search until the scorer is corrected.",
  "proposed_change": {
    "ops": [
      {
        "op": "set_weight",
        "name": "quality_score",
        "weight": 0.05
      },
      {
        "op": "set_weight",
        "name": "parameter_mean",
        "weight": 1.0
      }
    ]
  },
  "expected_benefit": "Reduces the undue influence of the mis-scaled quality_score so selection is guided by the valid parameter objectives (parameter_mean and parameter_spread). This should produce populations that move mean closer to 0.5 without being overwhelmed by the faulty quality metric.",
  "validation_plan": "Validator should observe that the optimization objective weights changed (quality_score down-weighted to 0.05, parameter_mean increased to 1.0) and that subsequent selection pressure is less dominated by the previous extreme quality_score values. Expected short-term signals: relative improvement in parameter_mean and parameter_spread metrics (or at least selection choices reflecting those objectives) rather than selection solely by huge quality_score values.",
  "confidence": 0.75
}</decision>

Reasoning: quality_score is returning massively out-of-range values (mean = 4.7597e8 ± 3.9802e7; best = 4.8517e8) while its documented range is [0,1], and its weight is 1.0. That corrupted, high-magnitude signal is likely overwhelming parameter_spread and misleading selection. The safest immediate action is to disable quality_score (set weight 0.0) so the run can continue using the reliable parameter_spread objective while the scorer implementation is fixed and candidates are re-scored.

